Orbit-Steel

Creative Catalyst: The genre was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule, salvaged from the wreckage of a failed 1970s orbital colonization attempt.

Orbit-Steel rejects the warmth usually associated with Calypso; instead, it adopts a brittle, freezing precision. The "Electric Sitar" is processed through time-stretched magnetic tape loops, creating a metallic, glass-shattering sustain rather than the expected folk-twang. The "Space/Orbital" atmosphere is not ambient, but percussive, mimicking the claustrophobic thrum of an airlock pressure gauge rather than the vastness of the cosmos.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 142–158 BPM; features a jittery, syncopated polyrhythm that mimics a skipping hard drive rather than the relaxed island swing.
*   Harmonic Palette: Relies on microtonal shifts and "negative harmonies," utilizing lydian-dominant scales that sound perpetually unresolved.
   Production Signatures: 1) Magneto-Phasing: Running signals through degaussed tape heads; 2) Vacuum Clipping: Intentionally starving the preamp of voltage to create a thin, piercing grit; 3) Data-Dithering*: The use of granular synthesis to turn vocal breath into percussive background noise.
*   Dynamics: Built on "Sudden Decompression"—long sections of high-frequency tension that abruptly drop into a cavernous, sub-bass silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The scene materialized in the abandoned lunar observation stations of the Andes, where scavengers—known as "Tape-Drifters"—spent years decoding the corrupted cassette discovered in the 1999 capsule. They wear heavy, pressurized diving suits adorned with oxidized copper jewelry and carry modified reel-to-reel decks as back-mounted instruments. Their ritual involves "The Calibration," a late-night gathering where the bass frequencies are cranked until the frost on the station windows vibrates into intricate, symmetrical patterns that mirror the orbital trajectory maps of the original astronauts.

Central Instruments: Electric Sitar (heavily gated), low-voltage vacuum tube oscillators, piezoelectric contact microphones attached to metal hull segments, and custom frequency-modulated drum machines.

For Fans Of: The Durutti Column, Konono Nº1, Boards of Canada, Shintaro Sakay.

